## What Problem This Solves

The core process supervisor (`src/process/supervisor/adapters/child.ts`) attaches `data`, `end`, and `close` listeners to `child.stdout` and `child.stderr` streams but no `error` handler. If either stream emits an error event (EPIPE from broken pipe, I/O error, resource limit), Node.js throws an unhandled `error` event and crashes the gateway worker.

Every supervised child process — all `supervisor.spawn()` calls — flows through this adapter.

## Why This Change Was Made

Add noop `.on("error", () => {})` handlers on both streams in the `onStdout` and `onStderr` setup functions. This follows the same pattern already used for `child.stdin` in `src/process/exec.ts:613`.

## User Impact

Supervised child process output streams no longer crash the gateway on stream errors. Data/end/close events continue to work normally.

## Evidence

**Regression test** ("suppresses stream errors on stdout and stderr"):

```typescript
const { child } = createStubChild(6666);
const adapter = await createChildAdapter(...);
adapter.onStdout(() => {});
adapter.onStderr(() => {});

// With the fix: error handlers installed, emit does not throw
expect(() => child.stdout?.emit("error", new Error("pipe error"))).not.toThrow();
expect(() => child.stderr?.emit("error", new Error("pipe error"))).not.toThrow();
// Without the fix: PassThrough.emit("error") with no listeners throws
// "Unhandled error" and crashes the process
```

```
node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/process/supervisor/adapters/child.test.ts

 Test Files  1 passed (1)
      Tests  19 passed (19)
```

## Risk

Low. Two one-line noop handlers. Same pattern already used in `src/process/exec.ts:613`.

